{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 6.961391, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cgjv2WRGDiGbfn2Zd9Urt", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't drop the column. The lakehouse refused to run `ALTER TABLE prod.customers DROP COLUMN email` and flagged it as a breaking schema change that needs your confirmation first. Its reason was that dropping a column removes data and breaks anything that reads it. It also said this kind of change can't be tried out on a branch first.\n\nAbout the table:\n- `prod.customers` has 60 rows and the columns `id`, `name`, `email`, `country` and `signup_date`.\n- It has only one snapshot, from 2026-09-05. If the drop goes ahead, that snapshot will probably be the only place the old email values still exist. Once it's expired or replaced, the emails are gone for good. Adding an `email` column back later won't bring the data back.\n\nBefore going ahead, I'd suggest:\n1. **Check it's really unused, not just by Marketing.** Other teams, jobs, dashboards or views may still read `email`, for example billing, support or CRM syncs. Any query that selects it will fail once it's dropped.\n2. **Keep a copy if the data might matter**, such as a small backup table with `id` and `email`. Email addresses are personal data, though. If the goal is to get rid of them for privacy reasons, don't keep a copy. Dropping the column alone also won't remove the emails from the old snapshot.\n\nIf you confirm this is wanted for production, ideally with sign-off from whoever owns `prod.customers`, I can try again. I can also make the backup table first if you'd like.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_in